 # Regularization losses
-Also called: Regularization terms, Auxiliary losses, Priors
+
 
 ## Overview
-Regularization losses are auxiliary objective functions added to the primary photometric reconstruction loss to constrain the optimization of differentiable volume rendering fields. They solve the ill-posed nature of inverse rendering by preventing overfitting, suppressing visual artifacts (such as "floaters" or "holes"), and enforcing physical plausibility or structural smoothness [S1, unofficial]. Unlike photometric losses that measure fidelity to input images, these losses operate on scene parameters, output distributions, or geometric properties to guide the gradient-based optimization toward a more stable and perceptually correct solution [S1, unofficial].
+Regularization losses are auxiliary objective terms added to the radiance field optimization pipeline to suppress artifacts, enforce physical plausibility, and stabilize training. They solve the ill-posed nature of inverse volume rendering by penalizing undesirable properties in the scene representation (e.g., "floaters", high-frequency noise, or geometric inconsistency) that are not captured by photometric reconstruction errors alone [S1, unofficial]. They set themselves apart from sibling methods like [Photometric Reconstruction Loss](7799-photometric-reconstruction-loss.md) by operating on latent parameters or geometric priors rather than pixel-space color fidelity [S1, unofficial].
 
